WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:03.184
Numbers on a spirometry report can look
intimidating.

2
00:00:03.583 --> 00:00:06.023
By the end of this lesson, they won't.

3
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:11.480
Pulmonary function testing is one of the
most powerful diagnostic tools in respiratory care,

4
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.880
and the RT is often the person performing
it, explaining

5
00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.180
it, and helping the clinical team
interpret it.

6
00:00:17.583 --> 00:00:19.543
We start with the fundamental
measurements.

7
00:00:20.166 --> 00:00:23.826
Tidal Volume, Inspiratory Reserve,
Expiratory Reserve,

8
00:00:24.106 --> 00:00:24.786
Residual Volume.

9
00:00:25.416 --> 00:00:28.496
Then the capacities, inspiratory capacity,
functional

10
00:00:28.496 --> 00:00:31.016
residual capacity, vital capacity, and
total

11
00:00:31.016 --> 00:00:31.656
lung capacity.

12
00:00:32.249 --> 00:00:34.470
Each one is defined, measured, and

13
00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:36.009
connected to its clinical significance.

14
00:00:36.583 --> 00:00:40.763
From there we move to the spirometry
values that drive most diagnostic decisions.

15
00:00:41.125 --> 00:00:43.166
FEV1, FVC, the FEV1 slash FVC

16
00:00:43.166 --> 00:00:43.546
ratio.

17
00:00:43.916 --> 00:00:48.896
This ratio is the cornerstone of
distinguishing obstructive from restrictive disease.

18
00:00:49.166 --> 00:00:53.246
When FEV1 slash FEC is below 0.70,

19
00:00:53.666 --> 00:00:54.666
you're looking at obstruction.

20
00:00:55.208 --> 00:00:58.428
When FVC is reduced with a normal or
elevated ratio,

21
00:00:59.028 --> 00:00:59.528
think restriction.

22
00:01:00.083 --> 00:01:03.603
The flow volume loop tells a story that
raw numbers can't.

23
00:01:03.916 --> 00:01:07.576
The shape of the curve reveals where in
the airway the limitation lives.

24
00:01:08.136 --> 00:01:10.676
Upper versus lower, fixed versus variable,

25
00:01:11.476 --> 00:01:13.476
intrathoracic versus extrathoracic.

26
00:01:13.875 --> 00:01:18.815
We'll also cover diffusion capacity DLCO
and what it tells us about the alveolar

27
00:01:18.815 --> 00:01:19.795
capillary membrane.

28
00:01:20.041 --> 00:01:24.921
Low DLCO is a hallmark of emphysema and
pulmonary fibrosis.

29
00:01:26.958 --> 00:01:30.218
Patient preparation, quality control, and
acceptability

30
00:01:30.218 --> 00:01:31.798
criteria all get covered too.

31
00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.809
A technically poor effort produces numbers
that mislead clinicians.

32
00:01:36.333 --> 00:01:40.013
The RT's job is to coach the patient and
recognize when a test is

33
00:01:40.013 --> 00:01:40.293
valid.

34
00:01:40.791 --> 00:01:45.391
Pulmonary function testing is where your
science and your patient skills come together.

35
00:01:45.708 --> 00:01:47.008
Let's build that skill!

